    "title": "Concept: Sex Determination at Fertilisation",
    "explanation": "<p class=\"text-sm leading-6 text-muted-foreground\">The sex of a baby is determined at fertilisation. An egg from the mother always carries an <strong>X</strong> chromosome, while the father's sperm carries either an <strong>X</strong> or a <strong>Y</strong> chromosome. If the sperm carrying an X fertilises the egg, the baby will be female (XX). If the sperm carrying a Y fertilises the egg, the baby will be male (XY). This results in a 50% chance for a baby to be either male or female.</p>",
